rFrom 5c0ee6c7aa12006d531c6d0726a1b6d53abcd71e Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Patch <patch@local>
Date: Mon, 12 Oct 2026 10:12:10 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] fix(ai): scan no longer fails with "Internal Server Error";
 redesign the app UI and add real-time feedback
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

## Why scanning failed (reproduced against a real Postgres first)
Two independent causes, both producing HTTP 500:
1. The database had no meal_scan_events table (migration 0003 was never run
   after pulling the AI PR).
2. A subscriber id cached in the browser (from the in-memory dev server) did not
   exist in the real database -> foreign-key violation.
Both hit the same line: the scan-event INSERT. That table is observability only,
yet the write sat on the critical path, so a LOGGING failure killed a successful
analysis. The raw SQL (with parameters) was also echoed to the browser.

## Root-cause fixes (backend)
- AiService.logEvent(): scan-event writes are non-fatal; failure is logged
  server-side and the user still gets their result.
- Global error handler: 5xx responses are generic + carry a requestId; the real
  cause stays in the server log. 4xx keep their helpful message.
- DomainError: routes previously turned EVERY thrown error into a 400 with the
  raw message (food-log, diet-plan). Now only expected business errors
  (DomainError) are shown; real faults fall through to the sanitizing handler,
  so a database failure no longer masquerades as a user mistake or leaks SQL.
- Startup schema check + /health: the server compares the tables the code
  expects with the tables that exist, logs a loud "run pnpm db:migrate" error
  at startup, and /health reports status "degraded" + the missing tables.

## Root-cause fixes (frontend)
- Self-healing identity: a cached subscriber id is re-registered idempotently
  (offline-safe) so a reset/switched database can't strand the device. If the
  server lost the profile but this device still has it, it is restored.
- openMealScan() now resets searchContext. Previously, scanning after using
  "Add item" on the Plan page silently added scanned foods to the PLAN, not the
  diary.
- Scan review converts the AI's estimate to grams up front. The store treats an
  unknown unit as grams, so "1 piece" would have been logged as 1 g.
- Friendly, actionable error messages for every failure class (offline, timeout,
  413, 429, 5xx); never raw server text.

## User feedback ("know what's happening")
- Toasts for add/remove/log/plan actions; skeleton loaders; guided empty states.
- Truthful sync indicator driven by the real Dexie queue + engine state, with
  auto-retry and "Sync now". Pending changes from an earlier session now sync on
  startup instead of waiting for the next edit.
- Scan flow: 3-step progress, real stages, elapsed-seconds counter, an honest
  "taking longer than usual" hint, and Cancel.

## UI redesign
- Design system (cards, buttons, inputs, motion, tokens) in globals.css/tailwind.
- App shell: sidebar on desktop; bottom tab bar + raised scan button on mobile.
- Rebuilt Dashboard (hero energy ring, macro bars, meal cards), Diary (week strip
  + day summary), Plan (day selector + daily summary — fixes the 7-column layout
  that clipped food names and kcal totals).
- Shared Modal (bottom sheet on phones), restyled food search.
- Removed backdrop-blur / large blur filters (expensive on low-end phones).
- Admin nav link hidden in production builds (route still needs real auth).

## Verification
- 104/104 unit+integration tests (50 + 54), incl. 8 new regression tests; each
  was confirmed to FAIL with the original bugs re-introduced.
- Real-browser end-to-end (headless Chromium) against the broken environment
  (missing table + unknown subscriber + no AI keys): 19/19 checks, 0 JS errors,
  and server-side psql checks confirm data synced.
- ESLint: 0 errors on all changed files. Type-check and next build clean.
